  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Sunday: A Low will push north-south through the \nregion over the period. It will push a strong dry cold front \nacross the Inland Northwest today. Main concern will be the \ngusty winds across the Basin. It will be followed by a deepening\ntrough on Saturday with cold air filling behind the front. By \nSunday, the low stalls over Southwest Conus and high pressure \nstarts to nudge into the region from the West. While most of the\nlowland locations will see little to no precip from the front, \nthe mid slopes of the Cascades and Idaho Panhandle mountains \nhave a 40-60 percent probability of at least a 0.01 of an inch \nbut 10-20 percent for 0.02 of inch for Friday. The Cascade Crest\nhas a 25-75 probability of 0.10-0.30 of an inch of precip. \nColder air will drop the snow levels from 7000-8000 ft Friday \nmorning to 4000-5000 ft by Saturday morning, leading to periods \nof mountain snow. Amounts are generally up 1-2 inches but \npockets of up to 4 inches are possible. Precip tapers off by \nSunday afternoon. Hikers and campers in the higher elevations \nshould plan accordingly. Friday highs will be in the 60s to low \n70s for most. Higher terrain in the Cascades will be in the 40s.\nSaturday and Sunday highs will be in the upper 40s and low 60s.\nOvernight lows will be in the 30s to low 40s. Many areas will \nbe in the upper 20s to low 30s Sunday night into Monday morning.\nIt will be a good to time to start putting away the hoses and \nsprinklers. \n\nWinds start to increase in this morning and peak in the \nafternoon to early evening. Sustained winds will be in the 15-25\nmph range from the southwest-west. Most of the region can \nexpect gusts of at least 30 mph. An area of the Upper Columbia \nBasin, stretching from the Waterville Plateau to Davenport, has \naround 50 percent probability of gusts into near 45 mph. There \nis still the 10-15 percent chance of 50 mph in this area. With \nthe prolonged dry conditions across the Basin, areas blowing \ndust are expected to reduce visibility and impact travel. An \nadvisory has been issued for the dust. Winds weaken behind the \nfront and switch northerly by Saturday morning bringing gusty \nnorth winds of 25-35 mph through the Okanogan Valley and \nWaterville Plateau. Breezy conditions continue Sunday with gusty\nnortheast winds across much of the region. Speeds will be \nslightly lower than Friday with gusts 25-35 mph across the Basin\nand Idaho Panhandle. Outside of the dust, other impacts include\ndifficult conditions for high profile vehicles and isolated \npower outages. \n\nMonday through Thursday: Ensembles are becoming better aligned \nfor the upcoming week. The Low is expected to stay over \nSouthwest Conus while ridging builds along the coast line \nthrough Wednesday. The Inland Northwest will be under a dry fall\nlike pattern with near normal temperatures. Highs will be in \nthe upper 40s to low 60s for most. Some areas in the lower \nCentral Basin could approach 70. Overnight lows will continue to\nchilly with a range of 30s to low 40s. There are increasing\nshower chances Wednesday into Thursday as another trough \npresses moisture into the region but confidence is low on \namounts. /JDC",
